## Bug Description

When calling `skills.update` via the Gateway RPC multiple times in the same session, the second call fails with:

```
Config validation failed: tools.web.search: tools.web.search provider-owned config moved to plugins.entries.<plugin>.config.webSearch. Run "openclaw doctor --fix".
```

The disk config does not contain `tools.web.search` - the error is caused by a runtime snapshot field leaking back into the source config during write-back.

## Root Cause

In `runtime-web-tools-BITgb3nI.js`, the function `setResolvedWebSearchApiKey()` deliberately mirrors the Brave web search API key to the legacy path `tools.web.search.apiKey` for backward compatibility:

```js
// runtime-web-tools-BITgb3nI.js ~line 1124
function setResolvedWebSearchApiKey(params) {
  const search = ensureObject(ensureObject(ensureObject(params.resolvedConfig, "tools"), "web"), "search");
  if (params.provider.setConfiguredCredentialValue) {
    params.provider.setConfiguredCredentialValue(params.resolvedConfig, params.value);
    if (params.provider.id !== "brave") return;  // non-Brave providers return here
  }
  // Brave falls through and also writes to the legacy path:
  params.provider.setCredentialValue(search, params.value);
}
```

The Brave provider is the only one that falls through without returning, so it writes to both:
1. `plugins.entries.brave.config.webSearch.apiKey` (new plugin-scoped path) ✓
2. `tools.web.search.apiKey` (legacy path) ← injected into runtime snapshot

When `skills.update` writes the config back to disk, `resolvePersistCandidateForWrite()` in `io-BxA9F2P4.js` computes a merge patch from `runtimeConfigSnapshot → nextConfig`. This patch includes the runtime-injected `tools.web.search.apiKey`, which did not exist in the source config. The legacy field is
